      "t": "Я решаю наши проблемы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Молодец, так держать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы хотите войну?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want a war?' A compact expression built around 'you' and 'the war'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Будет вам война.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There will be a war for you.' Notice the word order: 'will be' comes first, and 'a war' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Алло, добрый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, good afternoon.' Here 'hello?' sets up the idea, while 'day' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "За что вам платят деньги?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For what do they pay you money?' In Russian, 'what' leads into 'money' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы куда смотрите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are you looking?' The question word for 'where to' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'look'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В моём дворе мигранты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There are migrants in my courtyard.' Dmitry starts with 'yard' and pairs it with 'migrant' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Из ресторана Аркабалена.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'From the restaurant Arcobaleno.' Dmitry puts 'restaurant' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'Arcobaleno'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они режут живого барана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are slaughtering a live ram.' Here 'they' sets up the idea, while 'ram' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы там спите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And are you sleeping there?' Listen for the word meaning 'you' right alongside 'there' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Приезжайте быстро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come quickly.' Notice the word order: 'to come' comes first, and 'quickly' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Аркабалена.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Arcobaleno.' Listen for the word meaning 'Arcobaleno' right alongside 'Arcobaleno' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что ты делаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you doing?' The first content word means 'what', and 'are you doing' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я решаю наши проблемы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am solving our problems.' A compact expression built around 'I' and 'problems'. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Молодец, так держать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well done, keep it up.' In Russian, 'well done' leads into 'to keep' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
